#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087525 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087724 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6381faa4-5dfd-58fa-ace7-411fd2a97e98" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the x-Intercepts of an Ellipse By Completing the Square"}] :question/id #uuid "58d92951-d993-50ec-806c-802c627ada96" :question/math-academy-id "e-5213" :question/problem "![](images/45/4527514f6b522bf5922bcba4b77a1574b29f65c238c8b3dd976d6af6bb6c01ae.png)\n\nCalculate the points where the ellipse $x^2 + 3y^2 - 4x -  6y = 2$ (shown above) intercepts the $x$-axis." :question/worked-solution "To calculate the $x$-intercepts, we need to substitute $y = 0$ into the equation of the ellipse and solve the resulting equation for $x.$ We do this and solve the resulting equation by completing the square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+3{y}^{2}-4x-6y & =2 \\\\ {x}^{2}+3\\cdot {0}^{2}-4x-6\\cdot 0 & =2 \\\\ {x}^{2}-4x & =2 \\\\ ({x}^{2}-4x+4)-4 & =2 \\\\ (x-2)^{2}-4 & =2 \\\\ (x-2)^{2} & =6 \\\\ x-2 & =\\pm \\sqrt{6} \\\\ x & =2\\pm \\sqrt{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the ellipse intercepts the $x$-axis at $(2-\\sqrt{6},0)$ and $(2+\\sqrt{6},0).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087526 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087725 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "74a508c1-2379-590f-85f5-386a1990f137" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the x-Intercepts of an Ellipse"}] :question/id #uuid "a7fe6697-669c-57e2-8415-4bd40cf76dac" :question/math-academy-id "e-5214" :question/problem "Calculate the point where the ellipse $(x-2)^2 + \\dfrac{(y-2)^2}{4} = 1$ intercepts the $x$-axis." :question/worked-solution "To calculate the $x$-intercept, we need to substitute $y = 0$ into the equation of the ellipse and solve the resulting equation for $x.$ Doing this, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-2)^{2}+\\frac{(y-2)^{2}}{4} & =1 \\\\ (x-2)^{2}+\\frac{(0-2)^{2}}{4} & =1 \\\\ (x-2)^{2}+1 & =1 \\\\ (x-2)^{2} & =0 \\\\ x-2 & =0 \\\\ x & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the ellipse intercepts the $x$-axis at the point $(2,0).$\n\n![](images/db/db1ffc11b3321e87f7c5d2cb490d0381f4b950dfcfd64be00f49c07577f797c0.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087530 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087728 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a57ec9ff-99fd-5a3d-9a89-e76ded9d01cb" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the y-Intercepts of an Ellipse"}] :question/id #uuid "85e115e3-4f55-53e6-9d3b-decb3dc02267" :question/math-academy-id "e-5220" :question/problem "Calculate the point where the ellipse $\\dfrac{(x-1)^2}{4}+\\dfrac{(y-3)^2}{16}=1$ intercepts the $y$-axis." :question/worked-solution "To calculate the $y$-intercept, we need to substitute $x = 0$ in the equation of the ellipse and solve the resulting equation for $y.$ Doing this, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(x-1)^{2}}{4}+\\frac{(y-3)^{2}}{16} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(0-1)^{2}}{4}+\\frac{(y-3)^{2}}{16} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{1}{4}+\\frac{(y-3)^{2}}{16} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(y-3)^{2}}{16} & =\\frac{3}{4} \\\\ (y-3)^{2} & =12 \\\\ y-3 & =\\pm \\sqrt{12} \\\\ y-3 & =2\\sqrt{3} \\\\ y & =3\\pm 2\\sqrt{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the ellipse intercepts the $y$-axis at the points $(0,3 -2\\sqrt{3})$ and $(0,3 +2\\sqrt{3}).$\n\n![](images/c0/c090d0410cca8d00f0f93641a4f8bfbfa66c3b586894797e5c5c89291d11126d.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087531 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087729 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "fea426db-a854-5efc-a0cd-7d52019a7cc6" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the y-Intercepts of an Ellipse By Completing the Square"}] :question/id #uuid "5cbcffa6-eb3d-5438-ba08-4b47cbbf76ce" :question/math-academy-id "e-5221" :question/problem "Calculate the points where the ellipse $5x^2 + 2y^2 - 20x - 8y + 13 = 0$ intercepts the $y$-axis." :question/worked-solution "To calculate the $y$-intercepts, we need to substitute $x = 0$ in the equation of the ellipse and try to solve the resulting equation for $y.$ We do this and solve the resulting equation by completing the square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5{x}^{2}+2{y}^{2}-20x-8y+13 & =0 \\\\ 5\\cdot {0}^{2}+2{y}^{2}-20\\cdot 0-8y+13 & =0 \\\\ 2{y}^{2}-8y+13 & =0 \\\\ 2({y}^{2}-4y)+13 & =0 \\\\ 2[{y}^{2}-4y+4-4]+13 & =0 \\\\ 2[(y-2)^{2}-4]+13 & =0 \\\\ 2{(y-2)}^{2}+5 & =0 \\\\ (y-2)^{2} & =-\\frac{5}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $(y-2)^2 \\geq 0$, there is no $y$ such that $(y-2)^2 = -\\dfrac{5}{2}.$ Therefore, the ellipse has no $y$-intercepts, meaning that it does not intercept the $y$-axis at any point.\n\n![](images/dc/dc56aec7e7c16c4fcfee77f7126f53351c1513c4bfb5e64ac41834ed30ee5782.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186372335 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087725 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "74a508c1-2379-590f-85f5-386a1990f137" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the x-Intercepts of an Ellipse"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "dba08ba5-1b57-5a4c-bb76-40299b820f5c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "There is no $x$-intercept" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372337} {:answer/id #uuid "8cd7657d-8eec-59e8-a2b1-4e41b9cc76c7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(4,0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372338} {:answer/id #uuid "5adca319-de25-5651-a851-4419686ce657" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2,0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372339} {:answer/id #uuid "369f2c60-e233-559d-9ba8-bc96d6ced966" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-4,0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372340} {:answer/id #uuid "ad0770ec-1ac5-55a8-a993-81d5c375b952" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-2,0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372341}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5adca319-de25-5651-a851-4419686ce657" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2,0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372339} :answer-field/id #uuid "9a0cdef4-0076-5a2c-b898-5a1451899734" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372336}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "68f04bff-7cba-5650-a634-33f4c34b4531" :question/math-academy-id "q-16644" :question/problem "Calculate the point where the ellipse $\\frac{(x-2)^{2}}{4}+\\frac{(y+6)^{2}}{36}=1$ intercepts the $x$-axis." :question/worked-solution "To calculate the $x$-intercept, we need to substitute $y=0$ into the equation of the ellipse and solve the resulting equation for $x.$ Doing this, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(x-2)^{2}}{4}+\\frac{(y+6)^{2}}{36} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(x-2)^{2}}{4}+\\frac{(0+6)^{2}}{36} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(x-2)^{2}}{4}+\\frac{36}{36} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(x-2)^{2}}{4}+1 & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(x-2)^{2}}{4} & =0 \\\\ (x-2)^{2} & =0 \\\\ x-2 & =0 \\\\ x & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the ellipse intercepts the $x$-axis at the point $(2,0).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186401572 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087725 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "74a508c1-2379-590f-85f5-386a1990f137" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the x-Intercepts of an Ellipse"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0e455adf-7973-5ee4-bb3c-0bd83316580a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "There is no $x$-intercept" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401574} {:answer/id #uuid "d3568acd-90d7-5e24-95a2-7dddb6de4815" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(1\\pm \\frac{5\\sqrt{3}}{2},0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401575} {:answer/id #uuid "d33bd951-6cf6-508d-8421-15a11016a317" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-1\\pm \\frac{10\\sqrt{2}}{3},0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401576} {:answer/id #uuid "829db562-f4d5-5e31-b45c-b397742cb7e4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\pm \\sqrt{5},0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401577} {:answer/id #uuid "a69ed72a-53dd-5923-8c05-9efa0f54638b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(1\\pm \\frac{10\\sqrt{2}}{3},0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401578}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d33bd951-6cf6-508d-8421-15a11016a317" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-1\\pm \\frac{10\\sqrt{2}}{3},0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401576} :answer-field/id #uuid "d58b31e7-b6c4-5d90-9a58-b8914d59f384" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401573}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "ba3205c4-c133-5ac7-861e-655b59a14526" :question/math-academy-id "q-16645" :question/problem "Calculate the points where the ellipse $\\frac{(x+1)^{2}}{25}+\\frac{(y-1)^{2}}{9}=1$ intercepts the $x$-axis." :question/worked-solution "To calculate the $x$-intercept, we need to substitute $y=0$ into the equation of the ellipse and solve the resulting equation for $x.$ Doing this, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(x+1)^{2}}{25}+\\frac{(y-1)^{2}}{9} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(x+1)^{2}}{25}+\\frac{(0-1)^{2}}{9} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(x+1)^{2}}{25}+\\frac{1}{9} & =1 \\\\ 9(x+1)^{2}+25 & =9\\cdot 25 \\\\ 9(x+1)^{2}+25 & =225 \\\\ 9(x+1)^{2} & =200 \\\\ (x+1)^{2} & =\\frac{200}{9} \\\\ x+1 & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{200}{9}} \\\\ x & =-1\\pm \\frac{10\\sqrt{2}}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the ellipse intercepts the $x$-axis at the points $(-1-\\frac{10\\sqrt{2}}{3},0)$ and $(-1+\\frac{10\\sqrt{2}}{3},0).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088044 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087728 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a57ec9ff-99fd-5a3d-9a89-e76ded9d01cb" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the y-Intercepts of an Ellipse"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5265640f-aa03-53fe-80f4-4151a89672d8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,4.5)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282398} {:answer/id #uuid "af05336f-6415-5379-8ee4-84541cb5d527" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(0,4.5)$ and $(0,0)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283199} {:answer/id #uuid "b013fc84-d67d-51fd-b5f0-120b12ab066b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(0,1.5)$ and $(0,4.5)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283211} {:answer/id #uuid "b18e480b-d5c2-5d9f-9f7e-26df49f81f16" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "There is no $y$-intercept" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283225} {:answer/id #uuid "db06e7fd-4846-5a53-a36a-c492f73d0467" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,1.5)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283599}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b18e480b-d5c2-5d9f-9f7e-26df49f81f16" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "There is no $y$-intercept" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283225} :answer-field/id #uuid "1eda8e38-be54-599a-9c85-4cb20e3edabf" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283993}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "93774ab1-c034-5d25-91bb-1dbd3d1f509d" :question/math-academy-id "q-16682" :question/problem "Determine the points where the ellipse $\\frac{(x-3)^{2}}{4}+\\frac{(y-2)^{2}}{9}=1$ intercepts the $y$-axis." :question/worked-solution "To calculate the $y$-intercept, we need to substitute $x=0$ in the equation of the ellipse and solve the resulting equation for $y.$ Doing this, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(x-3)^{2}}{4}+\\frac{(y-2)^{2}}{9} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(0-3)^{2}}{4}+\\frac{(y-2)^{2}}{9} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{9}{4}+\\frac{(y-2)^{2}}{9} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(y-2)^{2}}{9} & =1-\\frac{9}{4} \\\\ \\frac{(y-2)^{2}}{9} & =-\\frac{5}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $\\frac{(y-2)^{2}}{9}\\ge 0$, there is no $y$ such that $\\frac{(y-2)^{2}}{9}=-\\frac{5}{4}.$ Therefore, the ellipse has no $y$-intercepts, meaning that it does not intercept the $y$-axis at any point.\n\n![](images/e2/e2d4eadef6206fee8006028477c30337e2019000c33ed971f1f048294ef72615.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088045 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087728 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a57ec9ff-99fd-5a3d-9a89-e76ded9d01cb" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the y-Intercepts of an Ellipse"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "13a9e09e-9301-51f8-a281-efd3bbfefdbc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281876} {:answer/id #uuid "89f445c3-e82f-5b4b-8249-7e3c7a6fcfce" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,-6)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282881} {:answer/id #uuid "a6fb24ea-6f90-5ddb-8b85-e425ce400f64" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(0,-3)$ and $(0,6)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283142} {:answer/id #uuid "bf7c0c61-2b94-5228-a190-1791bccf7863" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "There is no $y$-intercept" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283354} {:answer/id #uuid "ddfbe973-4f2d-596a-ba7a-ec092b4b796f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(0,-6)$ and $(0,3)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283627}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "89f445c3-e82f-5b4b-8249-7e3c7a6fcfce" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,-6)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282881} :answer-field/id #uuid "bb9266cd-e8dc-5824-b6cb-36a03fcd6e09" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284268}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "0590ee5b-b1c4-50b4-ad4a-94aa0c689fb0" :question/math-academy-id "q-16684" :question/problem "Determine the points where the ellipse $\\frac{(x-2)^{2}}{4}+\\frac{(y+6)^{2}}{36}=1$ intercepts the $y$-axis." :question/worked-solution "To calculate the $y$-intercept, we need to substitute $x=0$ in the equation of the ellipse and solve the resulting equation for $y.$ Doing this, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(x-2)^{2}}{4}+\\frac{(y+6)^{2}}{36} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(0-2)^{2}}{4}+\\frac{(y+6)^{2}}{36} & =1 \\\\ 1+\\frac{(y+6)^{2}}{36} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(y+6)^{2}}{36} & =0 \\\\ y+6 & =0 \\\\ y & =-6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the ellipse intercepts the $y$-axis at the point $(0,-6).$\n\n![](images/d3/d3780834bd9f303ee9d7a6fff0c1fcf28ccfff4cbe4d69c9ec38691a33aca7e1.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284650 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087724 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6381faa4-5dfd-58fa-ace7-411fd2a97e98" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the x-Intercepts of an Ellipse By Completing the Square"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0038c158-55e4-54f1-88ca-383efd36482d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\pm 3,0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281688} {:answer/id #uuid "0764f3b7-2620-505b-81bc-62a203c31a1d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(-1,0)$ and $(7,0)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281755} {:answer/id #uuid "c54652ed-3e65-5a88-9f0e-f066b10e10c3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\pm 4,0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283406} {:answer/id #uuid "d7e9395f-cb90-553d-b856-74844c7f2b44" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(-3,0)$ and $(4,0)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283571} {:answer/id #uuid "f2dfb1ef-f994-5be0-b856-7daf2cec239a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(-2,0)$ and $(3,0)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283812}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0764f3b7-2620-505b-81bc-62a203c31a1d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(-1,0)$ and $(7,0)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281755} :answer-field/id #uuid "68c9e310-dcaf-5da5-b540-bd9578a859da" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284108}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "ed1c7854-5567-5866-b98d-cc7ca4447491" :question/math-academy-id "q-170548" :question/problem "Determine the points where the ellipse ${x}^{2}+3{y}^{2}-6x+12y=7$ intercepts the $x$-axis." :question/worked-solution "To calculate the $x$-intercepts, we need to substitute $y=0$ into the equation of the ellipse and solve the resulting equation for $x.$ We do this and solve the resulting equation by completing the square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+3{y}^{2}-6x+12y & =7 \\\\ {x}^{2}+3(0)^{2}-6x+12(0) & =7 \\\\ {x}^{2}-6x & =7 \\\\ ({x}^{2}-6x+9)-9 & =7 \\\\ (x-3)^{2} & =16 \\\\ x-3 & =\\pm \\sqrt{16} \\\\ x & =3\\pm 4 \\\\ x & =-1,7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the ellipse intercepts the $x$-axis at $(-1,0)$ and $(7,0).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186292737 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087724 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6381faa4-5dfd-58fa-ace7-411fd2a97e98" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the x-Int