dinates of these points.\n\n$A($\n{{field-1}}\n$,$\n{{field-2}}$)\\,$\n\nand \n\n$\\,B($\n{{field-3}}$,$\n{{field-4}}\n$)$" :question/worked-solution "To calculate the $x$-intercepts, we need to substitute $y=0$ into the equation of the ellipse and solve the resulting equation for $x.$ We do this and solve the resulting equation by completing the square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{x}^{2}+2{y}^{2}+18x+6y & =24 \\\\ 3{x}^{2}+2\\cdot {0}^{2}+18x+6\\cdot 0 & =24 \\\\ 3{x}^{2}+18x & =24 \\\\ {x}^{2}+6x & =8 \\\\ ({x}^{2}+6x+9)-9 & =8 \\\\ (x+3)^{2}-9 & =8 \\\\ (x+3)^{2} & =17 \\\\ x+3 & =\\pm \\sqrt{17} \\\\ x & =-3\\pm \\sqrt{17}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, from left to right, the $x$-intercepts are\n\n$$\nA(-3-\\sqrt{17},0)\\,\\text{and}\\,B(-3+\\sqrt{17},0).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186403349 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087729 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "fea426db-a854-5efc-a0cd-7d52019a7cc6" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the y-Intercepts of an Ellipse By Completing the Square"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b9a9fd67-307c-5267-8145-f7345da1670e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,2\\pm 2\\sqrt{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403351} {:answer/id #uuid "e1be8d15-da88-5d38-9ea2-b30ea374905b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,1\\pm 4\\sqrt{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403352} {:answer/id #uuid "5f8ec0ac-dec3-5654-9e96-daab0e177436" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,2\\pm \\sqrt{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403353} {:answer/id #uuid "31530733-6b5e-5211-9161-51c9fb8276f0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,-1\\pm \\sqrt{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403354} {:answer/id #uuid "08832673-492e-50dd-ad4a-468502c295a0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,-1\\pm 2\\sqrt{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403355}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b9a9fd67-307c-5267-8145-f7345da1670e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,2\\pm 2\\sqrt{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403351} :answer-field/id #uuid "95684ce7-0099-5fec-8c6e-29f3ec84dc36" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403350}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "bb24f0ce-8ec6-592f-8577-163f3821ff7d" :question/math-academy-id "q-70185" :question/problem "Determine the points where the ellipse $4{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-8x-4y=8$ intercepts the $y$-axis." :question/worked-solution "To calculate the $y$-intercepts, we need to substitute $x=0$ in the equation of the ellipse and try to solve the resulting equation for $y.$ We do this and solve the resulting equation by completing the square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-8x-4y & =8 \\\\ 4\\cdot {0}^{2}+{y}^{2}-8\\cdot 0-4y & =8 \\\\ {y}^{2}-4y & =8 \\\\ (y-2)^{2}-4 & =8 \\\\ (y-2)^{2} & =8+4 \\\\ (y-2)^{2} & =12 \\\\ x-2 & =\\pm 2\\sqrt{3} \\\\ x & =2\\pm 2\\sqrt{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the ellipse intercepts the $y$-axis at $(0,2-2\\sqrt{3})$ and $(0,2+2\\sqrt{3}).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186403599 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087724 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6381faa4-5dfd-58fa-ace7-411fd2a97e98" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the x-Intercepts of an Ellipse By Completing the Square"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ef8edbea-4da2-562c-be95-1803e0e50af8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(5\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{3}{2}},0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403601} {:answer/id #uuid "e45d242e-ec58-51d9-b98e-0c301509d596" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(10\\pm \\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{2},0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403602} {:answer/id #uuid "9141967a-e95f-5916-97f3-21bd825b34c3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "There is no $x$-intercept" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403603} {:answer/id #uuid "3903afea-29b9-5179-b7c5-dc45513572f2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(5\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2},0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403604} {:answer/id #uuid "88c9f761-6944-512d-b426-301b6d2b5ff0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(10\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2},0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403605}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ef8edbea-4da2-562c-be95-1803e0e50af8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(5\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{3}{2}},0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403601} :answer-field/id #uuid "629087fb-db7f-51a8-827b-0862fb389bb9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403600}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "3538d2b4-fd15-5d04-9456-067812f7d246" :question/math-academy-id "q-72913" :question/problem "Calculate the points where the ellipse $2{x}^{2}+3{y}^{2}-20x+6y+47=0$ intercepts the $x$-axis." :question/worked-solution "To calculate the $x$-intercepts, we need to substitute $y=0$ into the equation of the ellipse and solve the resulting equation for $x.$ We do this and solve the resulting equation by completing the square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{x}^{2}+3{y}^{2}-20x+6y+47 & =0 \\\\ 2{x}^{2}+3(0)^{2}-20x+6(0)+47 & =0 \\\\ 2{x}^{2}-20x+47 & =0 \\\\ 2[{x}^{2}-10x]+47 & =0 \\\\ 2[(x-5)^{2}-25]+47 & =0 \\\\ 2(x-5)^{2}-50+47 & =0 \\\\ 2(x-5)^{2} & =3 \\\\ (x-5)^{2} & =\\frac{3}{2} \\\\ x-5 & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{3}{2}} \\\\ x & =5\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{3}{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the ellipse intercepts the $x$-axis at $(5-\\sqrt{\\frac{3}{2}},0)$ and $(5+\\sqrt{\\frac{3}{2}},0).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186403843 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087728 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a57ec9ff-99fd-5a3d-9a89-e76ded9d01cb" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the y-Intercepts of an Ellipse"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4a4e2843-1069-5d5a-bc66-06cba791d839" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,-2\\pm \\frac{4\\sqrt{3}}{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403845} {:answer/id #uuid "ee8990c4-efb5-5c40-8ee3-12b22397b390" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,1\\pm \\frac{4\\sqrt{3}}{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403846} {:answer/id #uuid "05b80062-5158-567e-9a1e-8cfd58f9c9b1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,2\\pm \\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403847} {:answer/id #uuid "d7aba08c-b98e-5626-b761-ad0e976d4fab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,-2\\pm 2\\sqrt{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403848} {:answer/id #uuid "4d59c083-d711-58a1-a0a9-72a889180cdd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,2\\pm \\sqrt{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403849}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4a4e2843-1069-5d5a-bc66-06cba791d839" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,-2\\pm \\frac{4\\sqrt{3}}{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403845} :answer-field/id #uuid "8df721af-644e-5936-b5f6-55c036b03023" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403844}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "c9bca935-b9bf-540f-a535-f5de3e3b987f" :question/math-academy-id "q-7642" :question/problem "Calculate the points where the ellipse \n$\\frac{(x-1)^{2}}{9}+\\frac{(y+2)^{2}}{6}=1$ intercepts the $y$-axis." :question/worked-solution "To calculate the $y$-intercept, we need to substitute $x=0$ in the equation of the ellipse and solve the resulting equation for $y.$ Doing this, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(x-1)^{2}}{9}+\\frac{(y+2)^{2}}{6} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(0-1)^{2}}{9}+\\frac{(y+2)^{2}}{6} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{1}{9}+\\frac{(y+2)^{2}}{6} & =1 \\\\ 4+6(y+2)^{2} & =36 \\\\ 6(y+2)^{2} & =32 \\\\ 3(y+2)^{2} & =16 \\\\ (y+2)^{2} & =\\frac{16}{3} \\\\ y+2 & =\\pm \\frac{4}{\\sqrt{3}} \\\\ y & =-2\\pm \\frac{4\\sqrt{3}}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the ellipse intercepts the $y$-axis at the points $(0,-2-\\frac{4\\sqrt{3}}{3})$ and $(0,-2+\\frac{4\\sqrt{3}}{3}).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186396141 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087725 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "74a508c1-2379-590f-85f5-386a1990f137" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the x-Intercepts of an Ellipse"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "02fe5fe7-b348-53e0-8947-15f0c37ed27c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\pm 3,0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396143} {:answer/id #uuid "2b6bcede-bd93-5e91-b587-867ae663acd1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\pm 2,0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396144} {:answer/id #uuid "6d0fc8b9-a163-539b-9c0f-8eb06fff83e1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(0,0)$ and $(4,0)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396145} {:answer/id #uuid "00403835-be99-5078-bb45-9f705d557d03" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(0,0)$ and $(2,0)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396146} {:answer/id #uuid "185228aa-01c2-5fd7-a5ac-217c7d571287" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(-1,0)$ and $(2,0)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396147}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6d0fc8b9-a163-539b-9c0f-8eb06fff83e1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(0,0)$ and $(4,0)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396145} :answer-field/id #uuid "384638bd-72a6-5df4-a17b-400ec33ad1b3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396142}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "85a05273-d7c5-5a81-9a38-af7c6efc1cfa" :question/math-academy-id "q-7643" :question/problem "Calculate the points where the ellipse \n$\\frac{(x-2)^{2}}{16}+\\frac{(y+3)^{2}}{12}=1$ intercepts the $x$-axis." :question/worked-solution "To calculate the $x$-intercept, we need to substitute $y=0$ into the equation of the ellipse and solve the resulting equation for $x.$ Doing this, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(x-2)^{2}}{16}+\\frac{(y+3)^{2}}{12} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(x-2)^{2}}{16}+\\frac{(0+3)^{2}}{12} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(x-2)^{2}}{16}+\\frac{9}{12} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(x-2)^{2}}{16}+\\frac{3}{4} & =1 \\\\ (x-2)^{2}+12 & =16 \\\\ (x-2)^{2} & =4 \\\\ x-2 & =\\pm 2 \\\\ x & =2\\pm 2 \\\\ x & =0,4.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186403857 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087729 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "fea426db-a854-5efc-a0cd-7d52019a7cc6" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the y-Intercepts of an Ellipse By Completing the Square"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d8ffd5a2-cc34-54ff-9dff-23dc300f6355" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,-1\\pm \\sqrt{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403859} {:answer/id #uuid "d0c7d047-707f-5931-9d5e-9a61b60ed7e4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,1\\pm \\sqrt{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403860} {:answer/id #uuid "f83190fc-dc05-547f-bf1b-da6929340b16" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,-1\\pm \\sqrt{6})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403861} {:answer/id #uuid "9180fb06-deed-5622-9444-9a79d51d72e5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,-1\\pm 2\\sqrt{6})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403862} {:answer/id #uuid "3643a336-82e3-5461-b332-ac90d59fc7d1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,1\\pm \\sqrt{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403863}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f83190fc-dc05-547f-bf1b-da6929340b16" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,-1\\pm \\sqrt{6})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403861} :answer-field/id #uuid "651c5733-a40e-59c7-b992-154c6de03a88" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403858}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d4ad8b92-e3b5-5a56-bfac-e69e0f571229" :question/math-academy-id "q-7646" :question/problem "Determine the points where the ellipse ${x}^{2}+2{y}^{2}-4x+4y=10$ intercepts the $y$-axis." :question/worked-solution "To calculate the $y$-intercepts, we need to substitute $x=0$ in the equation of the ellipse and try to solve the resulting equation for $y.$ We do this and solve the resulting equation by completing the square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+2{y}^{2}-4x+4y & =10 \\\\ (0)^{2}+2{y}^{2}-4(0)+4y & =10 \\\\ 2{y}^{2}+4y & =10 \\\\ {y}^{2}+2y & =5 \\\\ (y+1)^{2}-{1}^{2} & =5 \\\\ (y+1)^{2} & =5+1 \\\\ (y+1)^{2} & =6 \\\\ y+1 & =\\pm \\sqrt{6} \\\\ y & =-1\\pm \\sqrt{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the ellipse intercepts the $y$-axis at $(0,-1-\\sqrt{6})$ and $(0,-1+\\sqrt{6}).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088455 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087725 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "74a508c1-2379-590f-85f5-386a1990f137" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the x-Intercepts of an Ellipse"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "401bd850-0ffc-5b22-8ba0-6e49af7f33f4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(2,0)$ and $(4,0)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358299} {:answer/id #uuid "6b6f7637-b34f-56b3-8fee-c39a545e9fb1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(-5,0)$ and $(-3,0)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358694} {:answer/id #uuid "7047c2fc-0e0f-548d-bff5-dd12fc177d19" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(-4,0)$ and $(-2,0)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358728} {:answer/id #uuid "9e5f83a8-ea40-5b64-bb0c-83788a580759" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(3,0)$ and $(5,0)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359131} {:answer/id #uuid "f7eb0e02-023c-58bb-aac4-2dac5752d4d4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(-2,0)$ and $(2,0)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359948}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6b6f7637-b34f-56b3-8fee-c39a545e9fb1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(-5,0)$ and $(-3,0)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358694} :answer-field/id #uuid "c08fea00-5325-54f1-8a55-fe63ff389dc6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360327}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "8fc78834-5b61-5384-b707-af7562e6a703" :question/math-academy-id "q-9457" :question/problem "Calculate the points where the ellipse $\\frac{(x+4)^{2}}{3}+\\frac{(y+2)^{2}}{6}=1$ intercepts the $x$-axis." :question/worked-solution "To calculate the $x$-intercept, we need to substitute $y=0$ into the equation of the ellipse and solve the resulting equation for $x.$ Doing this, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(x+4)^{2}}{3}+\\frac{(y+2)^{2}}{6} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(x+4)^{2}}{3}+\\frac{(0+2)^{2}}{6} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(x+4)^{2}}{3}+\\frac{4}{6} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(x+4)^{2}}{3}+\\frac{2}{3} & =1 \\\\ (x+4)^{2}+2 & =3 \\\\ (x+4)^{2} & =1 \\\\ x+4 & =\\pm 1 \\\\ x & =-4\\pm 1 \\\\ x & =-5,\\,-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the ellipse intercepts the $x$-axis at the points $(-5,0)$ and $(-3,0).$\n\n![](images/f4/f491b886477531fbd081cbdd73244e9cf489bd2474528ea27b66b187b9eb170e.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360472 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087729 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "fea426db-a854-5efc-a0cd-7d52019a7cc6" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the y-Intercepts of an Ellipse By Completing the Square"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "66863bf4-62e4-5732-b965-4bad8ffca0df" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358650} {:answer/id #uuid "78d85a34-0cf1-508a-8269-098a65b24be0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(0,-1)$ and $(0,3)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358798} {:answer/id #uuid "99a45e86-0c4d-530f-8afe-f82b1377850f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,-2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359101} {:answer/id #uuid "c9b8eeb5-81d0-55e9-8a09-1d6ec5f635f6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(0,-3)$ and $(0,1)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359522} {:answer/id #uuid "dfc91c67-3097-5283-8f8b-3b8fe97cd689" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "There is no $y$-intercept" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359716}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "dfc91c67-3097-5283-8f8b-3b8fe97cd689" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "There is no $y$-intercept" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359716} :answer-field/id #uuid "5382299d-1f65-5550-87e1-91bfe7eccdd1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360123}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "81870e2c-d547-5f03-aff7-b63f30c1daff" :question/math-academy-id "q-9468" :question/problem "Determine the point where the ellipse ${x}^{2}+6{y}^{2}-8x+24y+28=0$ intercepts the $y$-axis." :question/worked-solution "To calculate the $y$-intercepts, we need to substitute $x=0$ in the equation of the ellipse and try to solve the resulting equation for $y.$ We do this and solve the resulting equation by completing the square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+6{y}^{2}-8x+24y+28 & =0 \\\\ {0}^{2}+6{y}^{2}-8\\cdot 0+24y+28 & =0 \\\\ 6{y}^{2}+24y+28 & =0 \\\\ 6[{y}^{2}+4y]+28 & =0 \\\\ 6[(y+2)^{2}-4]+28 & =0 \\\\ 6(y+2)^{2}-24+28 & =0 \\\\ 6(y+2)^{2}+4 & =0 \\\\ 6(y+2)^{2} & =-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $6(y+2)^{2}\\ge 0$, there is no $y$ such that $6(y+2)^{2}=-4.$ Therefore, the ellipse has no $y$-intercepts, meaning that it does not intercept the $y$-axis at any point."}]}
